description:
if/else in JavaScript works just like Python, but with curly braces {}.

    const age = 20;

    if (age >= 18) {
      console.log("You can vote!");
    } else {
      console.log("Not old enough yet.");
    }

Output:
    You can vote!

The condition goes inside ( ).
The code to run goes inside { }.

Now write a prompt asking Claude to write a JS if/else that checks if a number is positive or negative and logs a message for each case.

You can loop through an array with .forEach().

    const names = ["Sam", "Alex", "Jordan"];

    names.forEach(function(name) {
      console.log(name);
    });

Output:
    Sam
    Alex
    Jordan

.forEach() runs the code inside once for each item.
Each time, 'name' holds the current item from the list.

Now write a prompt asking Claude to create an array of 4 cities and use .forEach() to log each one.

Functions can take information in.

    function greet(name) {
      console.log("Hey " + name + "!");
    }

    greet("Sam");     // Output: Hey Sam!
    greet("Jordan");  // Output: Hey Jordan!

name is the parameter — it holds whatever you pass in.
You can use + to join strings and variables together.

Now write a prompt asking Claude to write a function called 'multiply' that takes two numbers and logs their product.

Arrow functions are a shorter way to write functions.

Regular function:
    function add(a, b) {
      return a + b;
    }

Same thing as an arrow function:
    const add = (a, b) => {
      return a + b;
    };

Shortcut (one line):
    const add = (a, b) => a + b;

return sends a value BACK to whoever called the function.

Now write a prompt asking Claude to rewrite a regular function as an arrow function and explain each step.
